
  Concretely, "add a userId parameter to [38;5;153mlogin[39m" is now probably the wrong shape for what you want. A parameter is
  something the caller already knows; a real user id is something the [3msystem learns[0m at login and then hands to
  everything downstream. So the design work is mostly about where that id is born, where it lives, and how later forms
  read it.

  First question — and it's the one everything else depends on. [38;5;153mlogin[39m at [38;5;153mapp.js:4[39m is a stub with a hardcoded
  [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m it never calls, so right now there is no source of a real user id anywhere in this codebase.

  - [1mKeep the stub, build the real seam[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m stays fake but becomes async and returns a [38;5;153m{ userId, ... }[39m shape from a
    fake backend. You get the whole identity layer, testable, with one clearly-marked place to drop in the real [38;5;153mfetch[39m
    later. Cheap now; the swap later is small and localized.
  - [1mBuild the real API call now[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m actually POSTs to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m and parses the response. Honest end-to-end, but
    it needs a real endpoint and a real response contract to code against, plus network error handling, and it can't be
    verified without a server.
  - [1mSource the id outside login entirely[0m — a separate "who am I" call after authentication. More flexible, but more
    moving parts than this app has any use for yet.

  I recommend the first: the persistence and consumption design is identical either way, and the stub keeps the work
  verifiable today without inventing a response contract I'd only have to redo.
